## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design dynamic data structures with tight amortized bounds in a streaming context, including handling duplicates and edge cases like K=0. It also probes practical trade-offs for sliding windows.

## Key Concepts
- Maintain counts and a running pair counter
- Special-case K=0 to avoid double counting
- Support batched inserts/removals within a fixed window

## Code Example
```javascript
class WindowPairCounter {
  constructor(K) {
    this.K = K; this.cnt = new Map(); this.pairs = 0;
  }
  add(x) {
    const k = this.K;
    if (k === 0) this.pairs += (this.cnt.get(x) || 0);
    else {
      const a = this.cnt.get(x + k) || 0;
      const b = this.cnt.get(x - k) || 0;
      this.pairs += a + b;
    }
    this.cnt.set(x, (this.cnt.get(x) || 0) + 1);
  }
  remove(x) {
    const k = this.K;
    const cur = this.cnt.get(x) || 0;
    if (k === 0) this.pairs -= (cur - 1);
    else {
      const a = this.cnt.get(x + k) || 0;
      const b = this.cnt.get(x - k) || 0;
      this.pairs -= (a + b);
    }
    if (cur === 1) this.cnt.delete(x); else this.cnt.set(x, cur - 1);
  }
  batch(addArr, removeArr) {
    for (const x of removeArr) this.remove(x);
    for (const x of addArr) this.add(x);
  }
  exists() { return this.pairs > 0; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if window size N changes over time?
- What are cache/memory implications when values are large or negative?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic skyline maintenance with batch updates, including amortized analysis and practical data-structure design.

## Key Concepts
- **2D dominance and Pareto frontier**: A point p dominates q if p.x ≥ q.x and p.y ≥ q.y with at least one strict inequality.
- **Invariant**: Frontier F is sorted by x with strictly increasing y, ensuring no two frontier points dominate each other.
- **Amortized analysis**: Each point is inserted once and removed at most once over n total points.
- **Space**: Uses O(n) space for all historical points plus O(|F|) for the current frontier.

## Data Structure
Use a balanced binary search tree (e.g., TreeMap) keyed by x-coordinate, with y-values stored alongside. This enables O(log |F|) insertion, O(1) access to the rightmost (largest x) point, and O(|F|) in-order traversal.

## Algorithm
1. Sort incoming batch by x-coordinate (O(B log B)).
2. Merge with frontier: for each batch point p, if p.y > current max frontier y, insert p and prune any frontier points with y ≤ p.y.
3. Enumerate frontier via in-order traversal.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Skyline {
  constructor() { this.frontier = new Map(); }

  insertBatch(points) {
    const sorted = points.sort((a, b) => a.x - b.x);
    let maxY = 0;

    for (const p of sorted) {
      if (p.y <= maxY) continue;

      for (const [x, y] of this.frontier) {
        if (y <= p.y) this.frontier.delete(x);
      }

      this.frontier.set(p.x, p.y);
      maxY = p.y;
    }
  }

  getFrontier() {
    return Array.from(this.frontier.entries())
      .sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);
  }
}
```

## Complexity Summary
| Operation | Cost |
|-----------|------|
| Batch insertion | O(B log B + |F|) amortized |
| Enumerate frontier | O(|F|) |
| Space | O(n) |

## Why This Is Asked
This problem evaluates understanding of dynamic tree updates and amortized analysis, demonstrating the power of subtree aggregation over naive full recomputation.

## Key Concepts
- Tree DFS preprocessing for subtree computation
- Subtree size tracking for batch updates
- Lazy aggregation techniques
- Amortized cost analysis
- Performance trade-offs vs complete traversal

## Data Structure Details
- **Preprocessing**: O(n) DFS to compute subtree sizes and initial S
- **Per-edge update**: O(1) using δ × size[c] aggregation
- **Per-batch cost**: O(B) where B is batch size
- **Memory**: O(n) for storing subtree sizes and edge weights
- **Optimizations**: (1) Batch processing to reduce constant factors, (2) Cache-friendly array layout
- **Comparison**: DFS recomputation costs O(n) per batch vs O(B) with aggregation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Preprocessing
const adj = [...];
const n = adj.length;
const size = Array(n+1).fill(0);
const w = Array(n+1).fill(0); // w[c] = weight to parent
let S = 0;

function dfs(u, parent) {
    size[u] = 1;
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
        if (v !== parent) {
            dfs(v, u);
            size[u] += size[v];
            S += w[v] * size[v];
        }
    }
}

// Update function
function update(edge, newWeight) {
    const [p, c] = edge;
    const delta = newWeight - w[c];
    w[c] = newWeight;
    S += delta * size[c];
}
```

Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a batch-friendly histogram with fast max-tracking, enabling threshold queries without rescanning. It mirrors real-world monitoring workloads where high-volume time-bucketed signals must be maintained with tight per-update costs.

Key Concepts
- Histogram bucketing and width trade-offs
- Segment tree for max-tracking over buckets
- Batch amortization and lazy refresh strategies
- Space efficiency: O(S) vs. O(N)

Code Example
```javascript
class TimeHistogram {
  constructor(T, W) {
    this.S = Math.ceil(T / W); this.W = W; this.counts = new Array(this.S).fill(0);
    // build a simple segment tree for max over counts
    this.seg = new SegmentTree(this.counts);
  }
  apply(t, delta) { const b = Math.floor(t / this.W); this.counts[b] += delta; this.seg.update(b, this.counts[b]); }
  batchUpdate(updates) { for (const {t, d} of updates) this.apply(t, d); }
  hasHeavyBucket(M) { return this.seg.max() >= M; }
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How does changing W affect S and latency? 
- How would you adapt for dynamic T or non-uniform bucket widths?

